      "narrative": "The copper lamps of the Finch gutter as rain drums against the shutters. Mara Venn turns from the window at your question, the collar of her charcoal coat still damp from the morning. She studies you a moment\u2014weighing something behind those sharp eyes\u2014then nods once and gestures to the scarred table between you.\n\n\"You want to know about the Gate.\" She pulls out a chair, sits, and folds her hands on the wood. \"I've spent three months picking apart its secrets. Here's what I can tell you.\"\n\nShe leans in, voice dropping low.\n\n\"The Ash Gate was sealed by order of the Kestrel Guild about six weeks ago. Official reason: structural instability. Unofficial reason?\" A pause. \"The lock was tampered with. Not forced\u2014*altered*. Whoever did it knew the mechanism well enough to change the sequence without leaving a mark. Toran Vex over there\"\u2014she tips her chin toward the nervous clerk at the bar\u2014\"maintained that lock for years. He swears it was sound the day before the seal order.\"\n\nShe lets that sink in. \"The Guild's official inquiry turned up nothing. But Old Bram\u2014the ferryman who worked the passage\u2014he told me something different the night before he died. Said he saw a courier in a travel-stained cloak pass through the Gate after hours, carrying a sealed message. The next morning, the Gate was locked, and Bram was found cold at his post.\"\n\nMara holds your gaze. \"I've been chasing that courier's trail ever since. Sable Quill, they call them. Seen near the Gate just yesterday, loitering like they were waiting for something.\"\n\nShe sits back. \"So that's the shape of it. A lock turned against its master, a dead ferryman, and a ghost of a courier who keeps circling back. What do you make of that, Kael?\""
    },
