      "narrative": "Mara's fingers still on the table. She holds your gaze for a long moment, then reaches into her coat and pulls out a folded scrap of paper\u2014worn at the edges, covered in cramped handwriting.\n\nShe smooths it flat between you. A name is circled twice in charcoal.\n\n\"Old Bram wasn't just the ferryman,\" she says, voice low. \"He sold passage. Had done for years, under the table. Anyone who needed to move through the Ash Gate quietly\u2014after hours, off the ledger\u2014they went to him. He set the price, opened the lock, took his cut.\"\n\nShe taps the circled name. \"I found a payment record tucked behind a loose stone in his shack. Five names, dates, amounts. Bram's handwriting. The last entry is from the night before he died.\"\n\nMara slides the paper toward you. The circled name reads: **Sable Quill**.\n\n\"The courier. Paid Bram twelve silver for passage through the Gate that night. And the next morning, Bram was dead, the Gate was sealed, and the lock had been quietly rewritten.\"\n\nShe leans back. \"So either Bram let someone through who shouldn't have gone, or Sable Quill did something on the other side that made sure no one would follow.\"\n\nHer eyes don't leave yours. \"That's the name. That's where the trail starts.\""
    },
